    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d438">

                <pb facs="0546" n="474" xml:id="pg474"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 15778/5–6.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Weitzel to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Panama</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1908-10-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October 6, 1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 363.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to advise, in continuance
                    of my No. 360 of the 1st instant relative to the fight between sailors and
                    natives in the city of Panama, that on Saturday afternoon a preliminary hearing
                    was had before judge No. 4 of the circuit, at which several enlisted seamen from
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">Buffalo</hi> and other witnesses testified with
                    particular reference to the identity of the person who is accused of having
                    stabbed Charles Rand, since deceased. As a result of their testimony one Jacinto
                    Escudero, who has been under arrest since the night of the disturbance, has been
                    charged with the murder and removed to Chiriqui jail for safe-keeping.</p>
                <p>I am sending herewith a file containing copies of reports, letters, affidavits,
                    and other papers relating to the case.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d438-f1"/></p>
                <p>No effort, apparently, has been made by the authorities to ascertain the identity
                    of the policeman who is alleged to have assaulted and otherwise brutally
                    mistreated the dying-seaman.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George T.
                    Weitzel.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
